          "text": "Yes—typically, a more detailed framing can produce a different and often better response.\n\nMore detail can change:\n\n- **Scope**: broad answer vs. focused answer  \n- **Assumptions**: fewer guesses by the responder/model  \n- **Tone and depth**: simple explanation vs. technical analysis  \n- **Recommendations**: generic advice vs. context-specific guidance  \n- **Edge cases**: more nuanced treatment if constraints are known  \n\nFor example, asking:\n\n> “Would X work?”\n\nmay get a general answer, while:\n\n> “Would X work in a small nonprofit with a $5,000 budget, no technical staff, and a three-month timeline?”\n\nwould likely produce a much more specific response.\n\nThat said, if the core question is straightforward and the missing context doesn’t affect the answer, more detail may not change the conclusion—only the explanation.",
